#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096431 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-405" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f02616ea8f44865a727bec3affe596503dbadacafb113acad6e5f755218b4ff5.png)\n\nFor the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral." :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096432 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" :question/math-academy-id "e-406" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum." :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = 4-x,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(4-(1+\\frac{2k}{n}))\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(3-\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096433 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-407" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}$ and $f(x_k) = x_{k}^2.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)= x^2$ and $b = 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" :question/math-academy-id "e-408" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x$ as the limit of a right Riemann sum." :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = x^2,$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "054d43cb-d637-5acf-ba8c-e957170e28fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247465} {:answer/id #uuid "0d68bdb3-37d6-567c-a5c7-49e43b6f2513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247545} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3f8575-eadc-538e-bf1d-5ffd8ace6426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249122} {:answer/id #uuid "e5731a80-e5b5-5cb0-a9bf-0807924353a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249445} {:answer/id #uuid "fb85a63c-400e-593e-bc73-1a7f7b612bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf3f8575-eadc-538e-bf1d-5ffd8ace6426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249122} :answer-field/id #uuid "760a5e1b-de5d-5f17-aa82-85090115f907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35148af9-fe39-53be-bd54-85b1a5b3aea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-113884" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{1+4i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c858a40c-f20b-55e5-b84b-e3d282235b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{9i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c858a40c-f20b-55e5-b84b-e3d282235b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{9i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232938} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e64ce4-dc0f-5c7f-8d0a-bb5a34372b24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bb0589a-33d3-5697-b41b-737b35b84cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-346174" :question/problem "Express ${\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,1/2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{1/2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{n}=\\frac{9}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{9}{n})\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{9i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(\\,1+\\frac{9i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,1/2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36401cbc-a3be-5f14-933a-695fb7aa1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36401cbc-a3be-5f14-933a-695fb7aa1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317738} :answer-field/id #uuid "426b9925-da9c-542a-ad67-7fc57c5f23ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9ab51aa-b753-59b0-bcee-a4fcc0134e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9ab51aa-b753-59b0-bcee-a4fcc0134e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318396} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde9f548-1427-5fca-b7bf-a0e28dfaf74b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91c3be0f-8f8e-52a1-9627-d3cf3af82a0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346190" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=5{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0c16e42-a6fd-5291-a75a-a545c6a864c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-49233" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\pi .$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{n}=\\frac{b-(-\\pi )}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=0.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={cos}^{2}{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{cos}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={cos}^{2}x$ and $b=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c11bc78-d2c8-508a-b8fb-990c82485b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(\\frac{4j}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339511} {:answer/id #uuid "672f2c27-138f-5c55-aef5-0b2361a5154e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-2+\\frac{4j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340041} {:answer/id #uuid "ab869e72-931c-52bc-97fc-2e5c42e4486e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340692} {:answer/id #uuid "ee951d44-ea61-556b-94d7-e1dd4325b6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341232} {:answer/id #uuid "eee0bce5-f344-5783-ba0f-f51aa7490a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(\\frac{j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee951d44-ea61-556b-94d7-e1dd4325b6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341232} :answer-field/id #uuid "a983d74d-4019-5c4d-aa85-405465fd5a9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f9a39ce-97fb-5b82-8335-1f0d956000c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73160" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-5{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=-4,$ and the upper bound is $b=-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-2-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516433a3-ddcf-5286-911f-d1661221641b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(\\frac{5k}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393266} {:answer/id #uuid "91a60e1f-ff52-5d12-8845-6de6b6f23736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393267} {:answer/id #uuid "6006a006-5f33-5ef4-b609-573993a53f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393268} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff05667-12f6-5f44-9206-bc6cf51be751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-{(-1+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393269} {:answer/id #uuid "af9dfa31-4950-5539-b718-f5c060dd0d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2(5+\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91a60e1f-ff52-5d12-8845-6de6b6f23736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393267} :answer-field/id #uuid "97365ecd-a18f-5653-861f-4053aa6db95b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5568099-5914-5fb0-903c-d4010e585001" :question/math-academy-id "q-73180" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=-2$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(-2+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
